<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alice Seeley Harris</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1870–1970</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alice Seeley Harris was an English Baptist missionary in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> whose photographs of mutilated Congolese, taken from 1904 at the Baringa mission, became key evidence in the campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a>'s regime. With her husband John Harris she showed them at meetings across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-reform-association">Congo Reform Association</a>, and she is seen as a pioneer of humanitarian photography.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alice-seeley-harris.jpg" alt="Alice Seeley Harris" width="200" height="268" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alice_Seeley_Harris_(20e_eeuw).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
